{
  "lessonActivities": [
    {
      "id": "lesson-1",
      "title": "After Certain Verbs: Gerund List",
      "content": "<p>Use gerund (verb+ing) after verbs such as admit, avoid, consider, delay, deny, enjoy, finish, give up, imagine, involve, keep, mention, miss, postpone, suggest.</p>",
      "examples": ["He admitted taking the keys.", "She mentions going to the gym."],
      "difficulty": 2,
      "audio": "after_certain_1.mp3",
      "functions": ["Recognize gerund verbs", "Form gerund clauses"],
      "tips": "Gerund often follows verbs about preference, avoidance, and mental actions."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-2",
      "title": "After Certain Verbs: Infinitive List",
      "content": "<p>Use infinitive (to+verb) after verbs such as afford, agree, arrange, ask, choose, decide, expect, fail, hope, intend, learn, manage, need, offer, plan, promise, refuse, seem, want.</p>",
      "examples": ["They agreed to meet.", "He refused to leave."],
      "difficulty": 2,
      "audio": "after_certain_2.mp3",
      "functions": ["Recognize infinitive verbs", "Form infinitive clauses"],
      "tips": "Infinitive verbs often show goals, intentions, or decisions."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-3",
      "title": "Mixed Verbs (both forms)",
      "content": "<p>Some verbs can take both gerund and infinitive with similar or changed meaning: like, love, hate, prefer, start, begin, continue. Learn how usage shifts context.</p>",
      "examples": ["I like swimming. / I like to swim.", "She started reading. / She started to read."],
      "difficulty": 3,
      "audio": "after_certain_3.mp3",
      "functions": ["Compare both forms", "Interpret meaning changes"],
      "tips": "Use available verbs in both forms to create nuanced sentences."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-4",
      "title": "Phrases and Prepositions",
      "content": "<p>Know that some structures use gerund after prepositions: after, before, instead of, without, in addition to, on, at. Also expressions: it's no use, it's worth, can't help, there's no point in.</p>",
      "examples": ["After doing homework, he played.", "It's no use complaining."],
      "difficulty": 3,
      "audio": "after_certain_4.mp3",
      "functions": ["Use gerund in prepositional phrases", "Master common expressions"],
      "tips": "Prepositions need gerund forms."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-5",
      "title": "Practice in Context",
      "content": "<p>Combine both patterns in story-based sentences with gerund and infinitive choices. Focus on meaning accuracy and natural tone.</p>",
      "examples": ["I plan to start running...", "He admitted cheating on the test."],
      "difficulty": 3,
      "audio": "after_certain_5.mp3",
      "functions": ["Contextualize patterns", "Create longer discourse"],
      "tips": "Always ask 'GERUND or INFINITIVE?' when choosing verb form after key verbs."
    }
  ],
  "writingActivities": [
